
```{r setup, include=FALSE}
library(DBI)
library(RSQLite)
library(dplyr)
library(dbplyr)
library(tibble)
library(libridate)
knitr::opts_chunk$set(echo = TRUE)

PSTAT132db <- dbConnect(
  RSQLite::SQLite(),
  "PSTAT132-db.sqlite"
)
```

1.
a) Compute total sales (SUM(amount)) and the number of order per product (product_id and product_name). Save the result to 'sales_by-product' (include product_name). Covert to tibble

```{r}
sales_by_product <- dbGetQuery(PSTAT132db, "
                               SELECT 
                               p.product_id,
                               p.product_name,
                               SUM(o.amount) AS total_sales,
                               COUNT(*) AS n_orders
                               FROM orders o
                               JOIN products p 
                               ON o.product_id = p.product_id
                               GROUP BY p.product_id, p.product_name
                               ") %>% as_tibble
```


b) Find products with average order amount (AVG) > 80. Save to 'avg_over_80) (include avg_amount). Convert to tibble
```{r}
avg_over_80 <- dbGetQuery(PSTAT132db, "
                          SELECT p.product_id, p.product_name, AVG(o.amount) AS avg_amount
                          FROM orders o
                          JOIN products p
                          ON o.product_id = p.product_id
                          GROUP BY p.product_id, p.product_name
                          HAVING AVG(o.amount) > 80; --using HAVING to calculate average after the orders have been grouped by product. HAVING filters those grouped results, keeping only products whose average order amount is greater than 80
                          ") %>% as_tibble()
```


c) Create a table 'weekly_orders' that aggregates orders (year-week), returning week label (ie. '2023-01'), n_orders, total_sales. Convert to tibble

```{r}
weekly_orders <- dbGetQuery(PSTAT132db, "
                            SELECT
                            strftime('%Y-%W', order_date) AS week_label, --extracts year and week 
                            COUNT(*) AS n_orders, --counts the orders in each week 
                            SUM(amount) AS total_sales --total sales for each week 
                            FROM orders
                            GROUP BY strftime('%Y-%W', order_date) --creates one row for each year-week combination 
                            ORDER BY week_label --sorts the weeks chronologically 
                            ") %>% as_tibble()
```

2. Given the following relations: STUDENT(StudentID, Name, Major) ENROLLMENT(StudentID, CourseID, Grade). Write relational algebra expressions for the following using the operators covered in class  (σ, π, ρ, ∪, ∩, −, ×, ⨝) to
a. Find the names of all students majoring in 'Mathematics'
b. Find all StudentIDs who are enrolled in course101
c. Rename the ENROLLMENT relation to E
d. Find the StudentIDs of students who are enrolled in course 101 OR course 202. 
e. Find the StudentIDs of students enrolled in course 101 but NOT in course 202.


**Relations:**

$$
\text{STUDENT}(\text{StudentID}, \text{Name}, \text{Major}) \qquad
\text{ENROLLMENT}(\text{StudentID}, \text{CourseID}, \text{Grade})
$$

**a.** Names of all students majoring in 'Mathematics':

$$
\pi_{\text{Name}}\bigl(\sigma_{\text{Major} = \text{'Mathematics'}}(\text{STUDENT})\bigr)
$$

**b.** StudentIDs of students enrolled in course101:

$$
\pi_{\text{StudentID}}\bigl(\sigma_{\text{CourseID} = \text{'course101'}}(\text{ENROLLMENT})\bigr)
$$

**c.** Rename ENROLLMENT to E:

$$
\rho_{E}(\text{ENROLLMENT})
$$

**d.** StudentIDs of students enrolled in course 101 OR course 202:

$$
\pi_{\text{StudentID}}\bigl(\sigma_{\text{CourseID} = \text{'course101'}}(\text{ENROLLMENT})\bigr)
\;\cup\;
\pi_{\text{StudentID}}\bigl(\sigma_{\text{CourseID} = \text{'course202'}}(\text{ENROLLMENT})\bigr)
$$

**e.** StudentIDs of students enrolled in course 101 but NOT in course 202:

$$
\pi_{\text{StudentID}}\bigl(\sigma_{\text{CourseID} = \text{'course101'}}(\text{ENROLLMENT})\bigr)
\;-\;
\pi_{\text{StudentID}}\bigl(\sigma_{\text{CourseID} = \text{'course202'}}(\text{ENROLLMENT})\bigr)
$$
